                                                                                                                                                                    <content:encoded >
                            <![CDATA[
                            <article>
                                <p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/crimson-desert/" target="_blank">Crimson Desert</a>'s Charting the Unknown has arrived, but rather than a focused story expansion, it's more about adding other elements to the existing world. </p><p>This includes endgame challenges, new housing options, a romance system, plus a sailing ship and ocean questline about exploring Pywel's treacherous coast and islands. Each feature has different unlock requirements, so here are the exact details for each.</p><h2 class="article-body__section" id="section-shipbuilding-and-exploring-the-ocean"><span>Shipbuilding and exploring the ocean</span></h2><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="7Cwz8Dd7sccFD6RWMsBv5g" name="crimson-desert-dlc-unlock-5" alt="Crimson Desert DLC - Shark" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/7Cwz8Dd7sccFD6RWMsBv5g-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="1" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline expandable" /><a href='https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/7Cwz8Dd7sccFD6RWMsBv5g-1920-80.jpg' target='_blank' class='expand-button icon-expand-image icon' ></a></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Pearl Abyss)</span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>You can unlock the ability to buy or build your first ship after "Expanding the Camp" in "Gathered Will" during the "Grounds of the Sunrise" Greymane faction quest.</li><li>The quest to build your first ship will unlock, and progressing this questline will lead to the new ocean story.</li><li>You can upgrade to a medium or large ship for 1.2 million or 6 million camp funds respectively, or build either, which will cost less funds, but more materials.</li></ul><h2 class="article-body__section" id="section-garden-of-trials-and-new-story-quests"><span>Garden of Trials and new story quests</span></h2><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="kmBFMrDPVVM2fesdYxUhCg" name="crimson-desert-dlc-unlock-4" alt="Crimson Desert DLC - Crab robot" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/kmBFMrDPVVM2fesdYxUhCg-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="1" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline expandable" /><a href='https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/kmBFMrDPVVM2fesdYxUhCg-1920-80.jpg' target='_blank' class='expand-button icon-expand-image icon' ></a></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Pearl Abyss)</span></figcaption></figure><p>You need to complete the main story to unlock the new Garden of Trials combat challenges. Once finished:</p><ol start="1"><li>Visit the Axiom Archive in the abyss, where you first met Alustin near the start of the game.</li><li>Turn right before the building's staircase and door to find a corridor into the new annex.</li></ol><p>There are also new story quests for Kliff, Oongka, and Damiane, which start after the main story is complete in Pailune and Demeniss, respectively, including new combat encounters in the world.</p><h2 class="article-body__section" id="section-romances"><span>Romances</span></h2><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="ga9zmJZ5JjZmKQsTku547g" name="crimson-desert-dlc-unlock-3" alt="Crimson Desert DLC - Romance" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/ga9zmJZ5JjZmKQsTku547g-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="1" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline expandable" /><a href='https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/ga9zmJZ5JjZmKQsTku547g-1920-80.jpg' target='_blank' class='expand-button icon-expand-image icon' ></a></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Pearl Abyss)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Kliff, Oongka, and Damiane can each now romance specific NPCs:</p><ul><li>You can check via an NPC's interaction menu to see whether romance is possible.</li><li>After you reach 100 trust, select the "seduce" option, then once likeability is full, you can "propose".</li><li>The four witches have new faction quests that let you build trust and likeability with them as romance options.</li><li>Characters you marry can follow you or reside at your house, and you can also divorce any NPC after marrying.</li></ul><h2 class="article-body__section" id="section-new-housing-and-management"><span>New housing and management</span></h2><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="Zii2fjKwxTw9HwPqG7fBCg" name="crimson-desert-dlc-unlock-1" alt="Crimson Desert DLC - House" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/Zii2fjKwxTw9HwPqG7fBCg-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="1" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline expandable" /><a href='https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/Zii2fjKwxTw9HwPqG7fBCg-1920-80.jpg' target='_blank' class='expand-button icon-expand-image icon' ></a></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Pearl Abyss)</span></figcaption></figure><p>There is a new upgraded housing mode in the expansion, plus facilities you can now manage:</p><ul><li>Unlock the Greymane Camp on Howling Hill and complete Luke's request in "Grounds of the Sunrise" to unlock new housing options.</li><li>Acquiring the Howling Hill camp will also unlock a number of "facility" icons around the map, which show what strongholds can be rented and used as new management facilities.</li><li>You'll need to liberate the region and complete its faction-related quest before you can access a facility.</li></ul><div class="product"><a data-dimension112="2e9a6354-d2fa-11f1-8a5e-51cd0df1dd69" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" target="_blank" rel="nofollow"><figure class="van-image-figure "  ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:661px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:98.94%;"><img id="6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd" name="kingdom come 2 square" caption="" alt="" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="661" height="654" attribution="" endorsement="" credit="" class="" /></p></div></div></figure></a><p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/new-pc-games-2026/" target="_blank" data-dimension112="2e9a6354-d2fa-11f1-8a5e-51cd0df1dd69" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" data-dimension25=""><strong>2026 games</strong></a>: All the upcoming games<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best PC games</strong></a>: Our all-time favorites<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-50-best-free-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Free PC games</strong></a>: Freebie fest<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-fps-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best FPS games</strong></a>: Finest gunplay<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-rpgs-of-all-time/" target="_blank"><strong>Best RPGs</strong></a>: Grand adventures<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-co-op-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best co-op games</strong></a>: Better together</p></div>
                                                            </article>
                            ]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                                </item>
                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ 'None of these people have ever made a game,' BioWare co-founder Greg Zeschuk says of game industry executives: 'They just don't know how it works' ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p>It's been almost 15 years since BioWare co-founders Greg Zeschuk and Ray Muzyka <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/ray-muzyka-greg-zeschuk-retire/" target="_blank">announced their retirements</a> from the videogame industry, and they've been resolute about staying out of it ever since: Muzyka continues to head up the <a href="https://thresholdimpact.com/" target="_blank">ThresholdImpact</a> investment firm he founded in 2012 and holds multiple roles at the University of Alberta, while Zeschuk owns <a href="https://blindenthusiasm.ca/pages/our-team" target="_blank">Blind Enthusiasm Brewing Company</a>, also based in Alberta, where he works with a small handful of other BioWare veterans.</p><p>But Zeschuk stepped back into the videogame spotlight, briefly, following last week's <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/rip-bioware-ea-has-shut-down-the-legendary-rpg-developer/" target="_blank">closure of BioWare</a> by parent company Electronic Arts, telling <a href="https://80.lv/articles/bioware-co-founder-greg-zeschuk-says-studio-was-killed-by-executives-who-have-never-made-a-game" target="_blank">80lv</a> that he got the news hours after it happened because he was on a fishing trip, and by the time he flew home a couple days later, he'd "already shelved it away."</p><p>"I wasn't there to share in the moment, which in some ways was nice because I was able to kind of just take my time with it," Zeschuk said. "I’m not saying it doesn't hurt and doesn't suck. And I think it's kind of sad. It's actually, in some ways, saddest for the city of Edmonton, where we're from, because we were kind of this beacon of hope and excitement and possibility. We showed you could do something really special in a place that was not known for doing things like that."</p><p>Zeschuk said he had his own "emotional moment" about BioWare years ago, when he left the company; his real concern now is for the people at the studio who now find themselves out of work: "Anyone getting put into the job hunt is a sort of terrifying endeavor and super sad."</p><p>He also said he has no regrets about the EA acquisition, saying it offered "stability" as the studio continued to grow and that "we saw there'd be value in being part of something bigger rather than going our own way and taking all the risks." At the same time, he had a feeling when EA was acquired by Saudi Arabia's PIF that it probably wouldn't go well for BioWare, because it doesn't fit into the guns-and-sports model: "BioWare makes these kind of unusual games that take a long time and they're complicated and so it's easier just to pump out sports games every year and that's a better business model in some ways."</p><p>Zeschuk was also critical of the idea, seen in the recent wave of studio mergers and AI-powered cutbacks, "that you can just kind of recreate great games with random people and AI. It doesn't work," Zeschuk said.</p><p>"The other harsh reality of almost all these companies, the people that go down the business track never really come from the development track. Because none of these people have ever made a game, like, never really sat and made one, like, from start to finish, even a small thing, they just don't know how it works."</p><p>Zeschuk does know how it works, and he was clear that he's not interested in going back. "For me it was time to go, and I kind of walked out of the games business without any intention of ever returning. I'm happy being a consumer and playing games," he said. "I have my opinions on how to make stuff, but as enticing as it would be, it's just not [worth] the personal struggle and cost and effort required to make a great game. Having gone through the crucible of game development, I'm not going back in there."</p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-ON92mW"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/ON92mW.js" async=""></script><div class="product"><a data-dimension112="a41265b8-d3e1-11f1-b559-0dfaebba918e" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" target="_blank" rel="nofollow"><figure class="van-image-figure "  ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:661px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:98.94%;"><img id="6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd" name="kingdom come 2 square" caption="" alt="" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="661" height="654" attribution="" endorsement="" credit="" class="" /></p></div></div></figure></a><p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/new-pc-games-2026/" target="_blank" data-dimension112="a41265b8-d3e1-11f1-b559-0dfaebba918e" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" data-dimension25=""><strong>2026 games</strong></a>: All the upcoming games<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best PC games</strong></a>: Our all-time favorites<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-50-best-free-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Free PC games</strong></a>: Freebie fest<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-fps-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best FPS games</strong></a>: Finest gunplay<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-rpgs-of-all-time/" target="_blank"><strong>Best RPGs</strong></a>: Grand adventures<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-co-op-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best co-op games</strong></a>: Better together</p></div> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/none-of-these-people-have-ever-made-a-game-bioware-co-founder-greg-zeschuk-says-of-game-industry-executives-they-just-dont-know-how-it-works/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ In a rare interview, Zeschuk said the closure of BioWare is sad, but his greater concern is for the people put out of work. ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
                                                                                                                                <guid isPermaLink="false">gQASqMQVbQAGgWk7BAc2rQ</guid>
                                                                                                <enclosure url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/SzmN5gBZ4AzPwnUJwehJHj-1920-80.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"></enclosure>
                                                                        <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 21:47:36 +0000</pubDate>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                <category><![CDATA[Gaming Industry]]></category>
                                                                                                <author><![CDATA[ andy.chalk@pcgamer.com (Andy Chalk) ]]></author>                    <dc:creator><![CDATA[ Andy Chalk ]]></dc:creator>                                                                                    <dc:source><![CDATA[ https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/hkTeZoDeGrvhQZtrNGPkbB-320-70.png ]]></dc:source>
                                                                <dc:description><![CDATA[ null ]]></dc:description>
                                                                                                                                <cf:isSponsored>false</cf:isSponsored>
                <cf:hasAffiliateLinks>false</cf:hasAffiliateLinks>
                <cf:isPaid>false</cf:isPaid>
                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:content type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/SzmN5gBZ4AzPwnUJwehJHj-1920-80.jpg">
                                                            <media:credit><![CDATA[Blind Enthusiasm Brewing Company]]></media:credit>
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:description><![CDATA[Greg Zeschuk]]></media:description>                                                            <media:text><![CDATA[Greg Zeschuk]]></media:text>
                                <media:title type="plain"><![CDATA[Greg Zeschuk]]></media:title>
                                                    </media:content>
                                                    <media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/SzmN5gBZ4AzPwnUJwehJHj-1920-80.jpg" />
                                                                                                                                                                    <content:encoded >
                            <![CDATA[
                            <article>
                                <p>It's been almost 15 years since BioWare co-founders Greg Zeschuk and Ray Muzyka <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/ray-muzyka-greg-zeschuk-retire/" target="_blank">announced their retirements</a> from the videogame industry, and they've been resolute about staying out of it ever since: Muzyka continues to head up the <a href="https://thresholdimpact.com/" target="_blank">ThresholdImpact</a> investment firm he founded in 2012 and holds multiple roles at the University of Alberta, while Zeschuk owns <a href="https://blindenthusiasm.ca/pages/our-team" target="_blank">Blind Enthusiasm Brewing Company</a>, also based in Alberta, where he works with a small handful of other BioWare veterans.</p><p>But Zeschuk stepped back into the videogame spotlight, briefly, following last week's <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/rip-bioware-ea-has-shut-down-the-legendary-rpg-developer/" target="_blank">closure of BioWare</a> by parent company Electronic Arts, telling <a href="https://80.lv/articles/bioware-co-founder-greg-zeschuk-says-studio-was-killed-by-executives-who-have-never-made-a-game" target="_blank">80lv</a> that he got the news hours after it happened because he was on a fishing trip, and by the time he flew home a couple days later, he'd "already shelved it away."</p><p>"I wasn't there to share in the moment, which in some ways was nice because I was able to kind of just take my time with it," Zeschuk said. "I’m not saying it doesn't hurt and doesn't suck. And I think it's kind of sad. It's actually, in some ways, saddest for the city of Edmonton, where we're from, because we were kind of this beacon of hope and excitement and possibility. We showed you could do something really special in a place that was not known for doing things like that."</p><p>Zeschuk said he had his own "emotional moment" about BioWare years ago, when he left the company; his real concern now is for the people at the studio who now find themselves out of work: "Anyone getting put into the job hunt is a sort of terrifying endeavor and super sad."</p><p>He also said he has no regrets about the EA acquisition, saying it offered "stability" as the studio continued to grow and that "we saw there'd be value in being part of something bigger rather than going our own way and taking all the risks." At the same time, he had a feeling when EA was acquired by Saudi Arabia's PIF that it probably wouldn't go well for BioWare, because it doesn't fit into the guns-and-sports model: "BioWare makes these kind of unusual games that take a long time and they're complicated and so it's easier just to pump out sports games every year and that's a better business model in some ways."</p><p>Zeschuk was also critical of the idea, seen in the recent wave of studio mergers and AI-powered cutbacks, "that you can just kind of recreate great games with random people and AI. It doesn't work," Zeschuk said.</p><p>"The other harsh reality of almost all these companies, the people that go down the business track never really come from the development track. Because none of these people have ever made a game, like, never really sat and made one, like, from start to finish, even a small thing, they just don't know how it works."</p><p>Zeschuk does know how it works, and he was clear that he's not interested in going back. "For me it was time to go, and I kind of walked out of the games business without any intention of ever returning. I'm happy being a consumer and playing games," he said. "I have my opinions on how to make stuff, but as enticing as it would be, it's just not [worth] the personal struggle and cost and effort required to make a great game. Having gone through the crucible of game development, I'm not going back in there."</p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-ON92mW"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/ON92mW.js" async=""></script><div class="product"><a data-dimension112="a41265b8-d3e1-11f1-b559-0dfaebba918e" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" target="_blank" rel="nofollow"><figure class="van-image-figure "  ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:661px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:98.94%;"><img id="6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd" name="kingdom come 2 square" caption="" alt="" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="661" height="654" attribution="" endorsement="" credit="" class="" /></p></div></div></figure></a><p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/new-pc-games-2026/" target="_blank" data-dimension112="a41265b8-d3e1-11f1-b559-0dfaebba918e" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" data-dimension25=""><strong>2026 games</strong></a>: All the upcoming games<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best PC games</strong></a>: Our all-time favorites<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-50-best-free-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Free PC games</strong></a>: Freebie fest<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-fps-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best FPS games</strong></a>: Finest gunplay<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-rpgs-of-all-time/" target="_blank"><strong>Best RPGs</strong></a>: Grand adventures<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-co-op-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best co-op games</strong></a>: Better together</p></div>
