                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ Patrick Stewart, Star Trek and Shakespeare legend, has died ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p>Patrick Stewart, the classically-trained actor who gained worldwide fame in the 1980s as Captain Jean-Luc Picard on Star Trek: The Next Generation and later as Professor Charles Xavier in the X-Men film series, has died. A message posted on Stewart's official X account said his passing came "following a brief and poignant journey with Alzheimer's Disease and Lewy Body Dementia."</p><p>Stewart joined the Royal Shakespeare Company in 1966, and took his first television role a year later on the long-running British series Coronation Street. He debuted on Broadway in 1971, and subsequently appeared in several feature films. </p><div class="see-more see-more--clipped"><figure><blockquote class="twitter-tweet hawk-ignore" data-lang="en" cite="https://twitter.com/cantworkitout/status/2115891266131300517"><p lang="en" dir="ltr">In lieu of flowers, memorial contributions may be made to:Johns Hopkins Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center Fund   https://t.co/luC5CdcWDyAlzheimer’s Foundation of America Caregiver Resources    https://t.co/zvNptfyuNdPhoto Credit: Tommy Garcia https://t.co/XNncEVq56X<a href="https://twitter.com/cantworkitout/status/2115891266131300517">October 29, 2026</a></p></blockquote></figure><div class="see-more__filter"></div></div><p>But it was his tenure as Captain Jean-Luc Picard on Star Trek: The Next Generation that propelled him to international fame. Where his predecessor, Captain James T. Kirk, was a notorious ladies' man of action and fisticuffs, Picard brought a decidedly more intellectual and thoughtful bent to the big chair that ultimately proved one of the show's most enduring characteristics.</p><p>Once or twice, the show even allowed him to stretch his Shakespearean legs:</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="high" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/45VAelC_QsI" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>At the same time, there was never any question that Picard could kick whatever ass was necessary whenever it was called for, and he carried that demeanor over to his portrayal of Professor X: A kind, patient father figure of immeasurable power, whose leadership was paramount despite his gentle nature.</p><p>He also famously kicked ass in Excalibur:</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="low" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/iIbBKRBAxcY" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>And Dune:</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="low" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/CnuaXR933mA" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>Stewart also accrued a number of <a href="https://www.mobygames.com/person/7757/patrick-stewart/credits/" target="_blank"><u>videogame credits</u></a> over the years in various Star Trek and X-Men games but others as well: He narrated Lands of Lore: The Throne of Chaos, portrayed Khelben Arunsen in Forgotten Realms: Demon Stone, and maybe most famously gave his deep, sonorous voice to Uriel Septim VII in The Elder Scrolls 4: Oblivion.</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="low" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/2xDm6zpBKm0" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>In addition to his acting accomplishments, Stewart was unwavering in his support of the LGBTQ+ community, and supported numerous humanitarian causes including Amnesty International, animal charities, and anti-bullying campaigns. He was knighted by Queen Elizabeth II in 2010, becoming Sir Patrick, which he described as "<a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/10217872" target="_blank">an unlooked-for honour</a>."  </p><p>William Shatner, who played Captain Kirk on the original Star Trek series, shared a heartfelt message of condolence on <a href="https://x.com/WilliamShatner/status/2115902238921228332" target="_blank">X</a>, calling Stewart "a friend, a colleague, and a magnificent actor."</p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:945px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:53.97%;"><img id="Vs6dXpxYv5mhTSqznaUFNc" name="shat" alt="What a heartbreaking week it has been. Sir Patrick Stewart was a friend, a colleague, and a magnificent actor. From the stage to the bridge of the Enterprise, he brought intelligence, grace, and a deep sense of humanity to everything he did. Captain Picard stood for the best of us, and so did the man who played him. I was filled with affection and admiration for him and his work.My heart goes out to Sunny and his family. He will be deeply missed." src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/Vs6dXpxYv5mhTSqznaUFNc-1920-80.png" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="1" width="945" height="510" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline expandable" /><a href='https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/Vs6dXpxYv5mhTSqznaUFNc-1920-80.png' target='_blank' class='expand-button icon-expand-image icon' ></a></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: William Shatner (Twitter))</span></figcaption></figure><p>Ian McKellan, Stewart's longtime friend both on and off the screen, paid his own heartfelt tribute, saying Stewart was "a man to rely on, to admire and in my case, love."</p><div class="instagram-embed"><blockquote class="instagram-media"  data-instgrm-version="6" style="width:99.375%; width:-webkit-calc(100% - 2px); width:calc(100% - 2px);"><p><a href="https://www.instagram.com/p/DfGRUe0B63I/" target="_blank">A post shared by Ian McKellen (@ianmckellen)</a></p><p>A photo posted by </p></blockquote></div><p>"He has been held in the warm embrace of family and friends, surrounded by live music, prayer, tears, laughter, and the kind of profound emotion that only he could inspire after a lifetime of devotion to us all," the announcement reads. "We will forever cherish the peaceful ease and reflection on his face, an enduring reminder of his invincible grace."</p><p>Sir Patrick Stewart was 86.</p> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/movies-tv/patrick-stewart-star-trek-and-shakespeare-legend-has-died/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ Stewart died at age 86 after "a brief and poignant journey with Alzheimer's Disease." ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
                                                                                                                                <guid isPermaLink="false">ieYgCBCYX3wzXPUUtx9bQe</guid>
                                                                                                <enclosure url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/WT5vdvXQX8KvB2w8eXv7Kh-1920-80.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"></enclosure>
                                                                        <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 20:33:11 +0000</pubDate>                                                                                                                                <updated>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:13:31 +0000</updated>
                                                                                                                                            <category><![CDATA[Movies & TV]]></category>
                                                                                                <author><![CDATA[ andy.chalk@pcgamer.com (Andy Chalk) ]]></author>                    <dc:creator><![CDATA[ Andy Chalk ]]></dc:creator>                                                                                    <dc:source><![CDATA[ https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/hkTeZoDeGrvhQZtrNGPkbB-320-70.png ]]></dc:source>
                                                                <dc:description><![CDATA[ null ]]></dc:description>
