                <cf:hasAffiliateLinks>false</cf:hasAffiliateLinks>
                <cf:isPaid>false</cf:isPaid>
                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:content type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/WT5vdvXQX8KvB2w8eXv7Kh-1920-80.jpg">
                                                            <media:credit><![CDATA[Getty Images - Photo by Monica Schipper/WireImage]]></media:credit>
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:description><![CDATA[BEVERLY HILLS, CALIFORNIA - JUNE 09: Patrick Stewart attends the 84th Annual Peabody Awards at Beverly Wilshire, A Four Seasons Hotel on June 09, 2024 in Beverly Hills, California. (Photo by Monica Schipper/WireImage)]]></media:description>                                                            <media:text><![CDATA[BEVERLY HILLS, CALIFORNIA - JUNE 09: Patrick Stewart attends the 84th Annual Peabody Awards at Beverly Wilshire, A Four Seasons Hotel on June 09, 2024 in Beverly Hills, California. (Photo by Monica Schipper/WireImage)]]></media:text>
                                <media:title type="plain"><![CDATA[BEVERLY HILLS, CALIFORNIA - JUNE 09: Patrick Stewart attends the 84th Annual Peabody Awards at Beverly Wilshire, A Four Seasons Hotel on June 09, 2024 in Beverly Hills, California. (Photo by Monica Schipper/WireImage)]]></media:title>
                                                    </media:content>
                                                    <media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/WT5vdvXQX8KvB2w8eXv7Kh-1920-80.jpg" />
                                                                                                                                                                    <content:encoded >
                            <![CDATA[
                            <article>
                                <p>Patrick Stewart, the classically-trained actor who gained worldwide fame in the 1980s as Captain Jean-Luc Picard on Star Trek: The Next Generation and later as Professor Charles Xavier in the X-Men film series, has died. A message posted on Stewart's official X account said his passing came "following a brief and poignant journey with Alzheimer's Disease and Lewy Body Dementia."</p><p>Stewart joined the Royal Shakespeare Company in 1966, and took his first television role a year later on the long-running British series Coronation Street. He debuted on Broadway in 1971, and subsequently appeared in several feature films. </p><div class="see-more see-more--clipped"><figure><blockquote class="twitter-tweet hawk-ignore" data-lang="en" cite="https://twitter.com/cantworkitout/status/2115891266131300517"><p lang="en" dir="ltr">In lieu of flowers, memorial contributions may be made to:Johns Hopkins Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center Fund   https://t.co/luC5CdcWDyAlzheimer’s Foundation of America Caregiver Resources    https://t.co/zvNptfyuNdPhoto Credit: Tommy Garcia https://t.co/XNncEVq56X<a href="https://twitter.com/cantworkitout/status/2115891266131300517">October 29, 2026</a></p></blockquote></figure><div class="see-more__filter"></div></div><p>But it was his tenure as Captain Jean-Luc Picard on Star Trek: The Next Generation that propelled him to international fame. Where his predecessor, Captain James T. Kirk, was a notorious ladies' man of action and fisticuffs, Picard brought a decidedly more intellectual and thoughtful bent to the big chair that ultimately proved one of the show's most enduring characteristics.</p><p>Once or twice, the show even allowed him to stretch his Shakespearean legs:</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="high" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/45VAelC_QsI" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>At the same time, there was never any question that Picard could kick whatever ass was necessary whenever it was called for, and he carried that demeanor over to his portrayal of Professor X: A kind, patient father figure of immeasurable power, whose leadership was paramount despite his gentle nature.</p><p>He also famously kicked ass in Excalibur:</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="low" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/iIbBKRBAxcY" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>And Dune:</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="low" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/CnuaXR933mA" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>Stewart also accrued a number of <a href="https://www.mobygames.com/person/7757/patrick-stewart/credits/" target="_blank"><u>videogame credits</u></a> over the years in various Star Trek and X-Men games but others as well: He narrated Lands of Lore: The Throne of Chaos, portrayed Khelben Arunsen in Forgotten Realms: Demon Stone, and maybe most famously gave his deep, sonorous voice to Uriel Septim VII in The Elder Scrolls 4: Oblivion.</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="low" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/2xDm6zpBKm0" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>In addition to his acting accomplishments, Stewart was unwavering in his support of the LGBTQ+ community, and supported numerous humanitarian causes including Amnesty International, animal charities, and anti-bullying campaigns. He was knighted by Queen Elizabeth II in 2010, becoming Sir Patrick, which he described as "<a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/10217872" target="_blank">an unlooked-for honour</a>."  </p><p>William Shatner, who played Captain Kirk on the original Star Trek series, shared a heartfelt message of condolence on <a href="https://x.com/WilliamShatner/status/2115902238921228332" target="_blank">X</a>, calling Stewart "a friend, a colleague, and a magnificent actor."</p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:945px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:53.97%;"><img id="Vs6dXpxYv5mhTSqznaUFNc" name="shat" alt="What a heartbreaking week it has been. Sir Patrick Stewart was a friend, a colleague, and a magnificent actor. From the stage to the bridge of the Enterprise, he brought intelligence, grace, and a deep sense of humanity to everything he did. Captain Picard stood for the best of us, and so did the man who played him. I was filled with affection and admiration for him and his work.My heart goes out to Sunny and his family. He will be deeply missed." src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/Vs6dXpxYv5mhTSqznaUFNc-1920-80.png" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="1" width="945" height="510" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline expandable" /><a href='https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/Vs6dXpxYv5mhTSqznaUFNc-1920-80.png' target='_blank' class='expand-button icon-expand-image icon' ></a></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: William Shatner (Twitter))</span></figcaption></figure><p>Ian McKellan, Stewart's longtime friend both on and off the screen, paid his own heartfelt tribute, saying Stewart was "a man to rely on, to admire and in my case, love."</p><div class="instagram-embed"><blockquote class="instagram-media"  data-instgrm-version="6" style="width:99.375%; width:-webkit-calc(100% - 2px); width:calc(100% - 2px);"><p><a href="https://www.instagram.com/p/DfGRUe0B63I/" target="_blank">A post shared by Ian McKellen (@ianmckellen)</a></p><p>A photo posted by </p></blockquote></div><p>"He has been held in the warm embrace of family and friends, surrounded by live music, prayer, tears, laughter, and the kind of profound emotion that only he could inspire after a lifetime of devotion to us all," the announcement reads. "We will forever cherish the peaceful ease and reflection on his face, an enduring reminder of his invincible grace."</p><p>Sir Patrick Stewart was 86.</p>
                                                            </article>
                            ]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                                </item>
                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ Modern Warfare 4's newest map is actually older than the Gen Z & Alpha teens who will play it ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p>After 23 mainline games, there are far more Call of Duty maps than anyone can reasonably remember. That's why I'm impressed it took just one screenshot for several fans (with back pain, presumably) to clock that an upcoming Modern Warfare 4 map is actually a remake of a map that's over 20 years old. </p><p>Backstab, the new 6v6 map arriving on November 6, is a dead ringer for <a href="https://callofduty.fandom.com/wiki/Burgundy">Burgundy</a>, first seen in Call of Duty 2 way back in 2005. That means Backstab is older than the teenagers currently playing Call of Duty—do with that information what you will.</p><p>A number of people noticed this around the same time, but I'll credit Reddit user <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/modernwarfare4/s/hxFsMsjkPw">wizward64</a> for providing helpful visuals.</p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:2560px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:36.02%;"><img id="8ZZjVCYN6Sif9QbnZDDxqc" name="burgundy backstab comparison" alt="Modern Warfare 4" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/8ZZjVCYN6Sif9QbnZDDxqc-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="2560" height="922" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Activision Blizzard)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Apologies to the reader typing "This was a classic, show some respect!", but there's no way I would've noticed the resemblance on my own. I played Call of Duty 2 on the Xbox 360 when it was new, but not all that much multiplayer. I really like the <a href="https://youtu.be/QQrsnytVwns?t=49" target="_blank">look of the original Burgundy</a>, though: The desaturated green grass and beige brickwork of the French countryside still looks so clean and attractive 21 years later. I'm also now wondering if my favorite Day of Defeat: Source map (<a href="https://dayofdefeat.fandom.com/wiki/Donner/Gallery">Donner</a>) snuck in some of these CoD 2 assets, or if all WW2 shooters of this era were riffing on the same Saving Private Ryan scenes.</p><p>The layouts are near 1:1, which is neat, though the theming is clearly altered. Burgundy was a WW2 map in France (duh) and Backstab appears to take place in either Russia or North Korea (I think I see Cyrillic script on those signs, so leaning toward the former). Backstab is also snowy, so maybe this town will exist in the eventual Russian chunk of the Hajin DMZ map.</p><div class="youtube-video" data-nosnippet ><div class="video-aspect-box"><iframe data-lazy-priority="low" data-lazy-src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/QQrsnytVwns?start=39" allowfullscreen=""></iframe></div></div><p>Burgundy was a pretty small map for Call of Duty 2, and I expect it'll feel even smaller at Modern Warfare 4's quicker pace. The map's signature feature was a pair of two-story buildings that overlooked the central courtyard from opposite sides of the battlefield. High windows with usable machine gun emplacements made these buildings important for map control, but those windows will presumably become regular sniper nests in Backstab.</p><p>Call of Duty is famous for recycling old maps, but a deep dive into the vault like this feels different from the usual. This isn't Infinity Ward remaking a popular map to please expectant fans like we see with yearly iterations of Nuketown and Shipment, but instead pulling a relatively unknown goodie and refreshing it for a new audience. </p><p>The OG Call of Duty studio has apparently been doing this for a while and I didn't notice: As commenters point out in that same Reddit thread, the Modern Warfare 2 (2022) map <a href="https://callofduty.fandom.com/wiki/Taraq">Taraq</a> was a remake of CoD 1's <a href="https://callofduty.fandom.com/wiki/Neuville">Neuville</a> and Modern Warfare 2019 map <a href="https://callofduty.fandom.com/wiki/Suldal_Harbor">Suldal Harbor</a> was a remake of CoD 1's <a href="https://callofduty.fandom.com/wiki/Harbor">Harbor</a>. Hey, those maps were pretty good.</p><div class="product"><a data-dimension112="e13686bc-d3ce-11f1-b768-a7a6c08becad" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" target="_blank" rel="nofollow"><figure class="van-image-figure "  ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:661px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:98.94%;"><img id="6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd" name="kingdom come 2 square" caption="" alt="" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="661" height="654" attribution="" endorsement="" credit="" class="" /></p></div></div></figure></a><p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/new-pc-games-2026/" target="_blank" data-dimension112="e13686bc-d3ce-11f1-b768-a7a6c08becad" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" data-dimension25=""><strong>2026 games</strong></a>: All the upcoming games<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best PC games</strong></a>: Our all-time favorites<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-50-best-free-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Free PC games</strong></a>: Freebie fest<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-fps-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best FPS games</strong></a>: Finest gunplay<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-rpgs-of-all-time/" target="_blank"><strong>Best RPGs</strong></a>: Grand adventures<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-co-op-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best co-op games</strong></a>: Better together</p></div> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/games/call-of-duty/modern-warfare-4s-newest-map-is-actually-older-than-the-gen-z-and-alpha-teens-who-will-play-it/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ This map can drink. ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
