                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ $60 million AI rack cluster listed on eBay, with 1.4 petabytes of memory and almost 10,000 Nvidia AI GPUs ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p>Who said those big AI companies have to get all the gear? If you had a cool $60 million hanging around yesterday, you could have picked up an AI supercomputer for yourself, complete with thousands of Nvidia A100 GPUs and AMD EPYC CPUs split over 300 racks. And if you went cheap, you could even get shipping for free. </p><p>Core 4 Solutions, a company that sells IT parts to companies like Amazon, Lucasfilm, Microsoft, Meta and even the US Department of Justice, <a href="https://www.ebay.com/itm/880041409723" target="_blank">listed the 300-rack cluster</a> on its page this week (via <a href="https://www.tomshardware.com/desktops/servers/it-vendor-lists-massive-usd60-million-ai-cluster-on-ebay-300x-racks-of-decommissioned-gpus-includes-9-600-nvidia-a100-gpus-and-4-200-amd-epyc-cpus-plus-free-shipping" target="_blank">Tom's Hardware</a>).</p><p>That listing says each rack includes four compute nodes, complete with eight Nvidia A100 40 GB GPUs, eight Nvidia Connect network interface cards, and two AMD EPYC 64-core CPUs. Each rack also comes with 1 TB of DDR4 memory and three 1U management nodes, complete with two EPYC CPUs. As pointed out by Tom's Hardware, this equates to 9,600 GPUs and 4200 CPUs. </p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-OKyGme"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/OKyGme.js" async=""></script><p>The Nvidia A100 GPU is a now-discontinued enterprise-level GPU built on the Ampere architecture, and it's specifically designed for AI use cases and data analytics. You are getting 6912 CUDA cores per GPU (A little more than the <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/graphics-cards/nvidia-rtx-5070-founders-edition-review/" target="_blank">RTX 5070</a> has), though it's not something one would use for gaming. </p><p>The $59.7 million listing ended today, October 28, but it's not clear if the 300 racks were actually sold. If you happen to live in the United States, you could even get the racks sent free shipping if you chose the FedEx Ground or FedEx Home Delivery options. What a bargain. </p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="zXmMtEa34q8c6PDMf8LjrC" name="C - 2026-10-29T131736.568" alt="A listing of a $59.7 million rack cluster on ebay from Core 4 Solutions" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/zXmMtEa34q8c6PDMf8LjrC-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Core 4 Solutions on eBay)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Core 4 Solutions is selling some servers on <a href="https://www.core4solutions.com/" target="_blank">its website</a>, but the <a href="https://www.core4solutions.com/products/c880-m4-v3-8s-2tb-32gb-dimms" target="_blank">most expensive of these only fetches $87K</a>. The 300 racks on eBay are almost 700 times more expensive. </p><p>Impressively, the $59.7 million rack cluster has a whopping 1.4 petabytes of memory, which is enough for just under 90,000 relatively impressive gaming rigs (assuming <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/memory/my-testing-shows-that-16-gb-of-system-memory-is-still-absolutely-fine-for-todays-pc-games-but-there-are-some-caveats-to-it-all/" target="_blank">16 GB is enough</a>). </p><p>So, if you're wondering why your RAM is so costly, you can thank AI servers (and AI companies, by extension) for sucking up all that sweet, sweet memory. It really puts into perspective just how much AI companies are spending on all their gear—<a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/nvidia-and-amd-invest-billions-into-meta-as-companies-egg-each-other-on-to-bet-even-more-on-ai/" target="_blank">and each other</a>.</p> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/usd60-million-ai-rack-cluster-listed-on-ebay-with-1-4-petabytes-of-memory-and-almost-10-000-nvidia-ai-gpus/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ And it even had free shipping. ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
                                                                                                                                <guid isPermaLink="false">CeBYKF6AKZrjo2dPVyYFTk</guid>
                                                                                                <enclosure url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/jcGVMaYBM7XvknNbUmJcxn-1920-80.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"></enclosure>
                                                                        <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:01:48 +0000</pubDate>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                <category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
                                                    <category><![CDATA[Software]]></category>
                                                                                                                    <dc:creator><![CDATA[ James Bentley ]]></dc:creator>                                                                                    <dc:source><![CDATA[ https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/PVsHAkx27zJptZHndizEAE-320-70.png ]]></dc:source>
                                                                <dc:description><![CDATA[ &lt;p&gt;James is a more recent PC gaming convert, often admiring graphics cards, cases, and motherboards from afar. It was not until 2019, after just finishing a degree in law and media, that they decided to throw out the last few years of education, build their PC, and start writing about gaming instead. In that time, he has covered the latest doodads, contraptions, and gismos, and loved every second of it. Hey, it’s better than writing case briefs.&lt;/p&gt; ]]></dc:description>
                                                                                                                                <cf:isSponsored>false</cf:isSponsored>
                <cf:hasAffiliateLinks>false</cf:hasAffiliateLinks>
                <cf:isPaid>false</cf:isPaid>
                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:content type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/jcGVMaYBM7XvknNbUmJcxn-1920-80.jpg">
                                                            <media:credit><![CDATA[Nvidia]]></media:credit>
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:description><![CDATA[Nvidia data center]]></media:description>                                                            <media:text><![CDATA[Nvidia data center]]></media:text>
                                <media:title type="plain"><![CDATA[Nvidia data center]]></media:title>
                                                    </media:content>
                                                    <media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/jcGVMaYBM7XvknNbUmJcxn-1920-80.jpg" />
                                                                                                                                                                    <content:encoded >
                            <![CDATA[
                            <article>
                                <p>Who said those big AI companies have to get all the gear? If you had a cool $60 million hanging around yesterday, you could have picked up an AI supercomputer for yourself, complete with thousands of Nvidia A100 GPUs and AMD EPYC CPUs split over 300 racks. And if you went cheap, you could even get shipping for free. </p><p>Core 4 Solutions, a company that sells IT parts to companies like Amazon, Lucasfilm, Microsoft, Meta and even the US Department of Justice, <a href="https://www.ebay.com/itm/880041409723" target="_blank">listed the 300-rack cluster</a> on its page this week (via <a href="https://www.tomshardware.com/desktops/servers/it-vendor-lists-massive-usd60-million-ai-cluster-on-ebay-300x-racks-of-decommissioned-gpus-includes-9-600-nvidia-a100-gpus-and-4-200-amd-epyc-cpus-plus-free-shipping" target="_blank">Tom's Hardware</a>).</p><p>That listing says each rack includes four compute nodes, complete with eight Nvidia A100 40 GB GPUs, eight Nvidia Connect network interface cards, and two AMD EPYC 64-core CPUs. Each rack also comes with 1 TB of DDR4 memory and three 1U management nodes, complete with two EPYC CPUs. As pointed out by Tom's Hardware, this equates to 9,600 GPUs and 4200 CPUs. </p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-OKyGme"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/OKyGme.js" async=""></script><p>The Nvidia A100 GPU is a now-discontinued enterprise-level GPU built on the Ampere architecture, and it's specifically designed for AI use cases and data analytics. You are getting 6912 CUDA cores per GPU (A little more than the <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/graphics-cards/nvidia-rtx-5070-founders-edition-review/" target="_blank">RTX 5070</a> has), though it's not something one would use for gaming. </p><p>The $59.7 million listing ended today, October 28, but it's not clear if the 300 racks were actually sold. If you happen to live in the United States, you could even get the racks sent free shipping if you chose the FedEx Ground or FedEx Home Delivery options. What a bargain. </p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="zXmMtEa34q8c6PDMf8LjrC" name="C - 2026-10-29T131736.568" alt="A listing of a $59.7 million rack cluster on ebay from Core 4 Solutions" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/zXmMtEa34q8c6PDMf8LjrC-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Core 4 Solutions on eBay)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Core 4 Solutions is selling some servers on <a href="https://www.core4solutions.com/" target="_blank">its website</a>, but the <a href="https://www.core4solutions.com/products/c880-m4-v3-8s-2tb-32gb-dimms" target="_blank">most expensive of these only fetches $87K</a>. The 300 racks on eBay are almost 700 times more expensive. </p><p>Impressively, the $59.7 million rack cluster has a whopping 1.4 petabytes of memory, which is enough for just under 90,000 relatively impressive gaming rigs (assuming <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/memory/my-testing-shows-that-16-gb-of-system-memory-is-still-absolutely-fine-for-todays-pc-games-but-there-are-some-caveats-to-it-all/" target="_blank">16 GB is enough</a>). </p><p>So, if you're wondering why your RAM is so costly, you can thank AI servers (and AI companies, by extension) for sucking up all that sweet, sweet memory. It really puts into perspective just how much AI companies are spending on all their gear—<a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/nvidia-and-amd-invest-billions-into-meta-as-companies-egg-each-other-on-to-bet-even-more-on-ai/" target="_blank">and each other</a>.</p>
                                                            </article>
                            ]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                                </item>
                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ If Kane Parsons does make a Backrooms sequel then it'll be to 'poke at' aspects of the first film that 'didn't feel like it accurately spoke to the cultural core of Backrooms' ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p>The Backrooms left me with more questions than answers, which to be fair <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/horror/the-backrooms-film-left-me-with-more-questions-than-answers-and-thats-why-its-the-perfect-adaptation/">is exactly what I wanted from it</a>. But, while I enjoy sustaining the mystique of this odd liminal space, I would like to see a bit more of what Parsons has to offer of his take on the backrooms—so yes, I would like a sequel. </p><p>It's still touch and go whether Parsons will even revisit The Backrooms on the silver screen, but if he did there are  a couple of things he'd apparently do differently: </p><p>"I consider it, me advancing and doubling down on the strengths that I think are more universally recognised about the core idea," Parsons says in an <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1LemQQUuSWI" target="_blank">interview with The Kyle Buchanan Show</a>. "Rather than doubling down on the things or points where it didn't feel like it accurately spoke to the cultural core of Backrooms in the first film—[there are] a few little places here and there, that I would personally go out of my way to poke at a little bit." </p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1825px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.27%;"><img id="fx4CCupzbKBSW8inU8VyLg" name="https___cdn.sanity.io_images_xq1bjtf4_production_9b86288974790bf021f03df0ad1c25108d554f79-1825x1027" alt="Chiwetel Ejiofor looking through a passageway in a yellow office" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/fx4CCupzbKBSW8inU8VyLg-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="1825" height="1027" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: A24)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Instead of approaching a sequel in the traditional sense of a linear continuation of the story, Parsons may try something different , like "an expanding image rather than a line". A sequel would simply serve to add more flavour to the universe like "a different branch on the tree" instead of strictly following the same story and characters from the first movie. It seems that Parsons wishes to add more life to his version of the backrooms in this way, so the viewer can decide how much they wish to watch and "when to stop".</p><p>Parsons  also hints at the fact that there may be other characters, either new or from his YouTube series, that "are still lying in wait" and will probably make an appearance "in the near future". But this may all be in the form of a YouTube video instead of a full-blown movie as we also know Parsons is working on a project exclusively for YouTube at this moment. </p><p>Ultimately, though, this is all just theories and abstract ideas, as Parsons doesn't confirm if a sequel is even in the works "because I like being subversive, and the excitement comes from not sharing—so I can't say."</p><div class="product"><a data-dimension112="2ee38946-d39d-11f1-aceb-d12e585933c9" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" target="_blank" rel="nofollow"><figure class="van-image-figure "  ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:661px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:98.94%;"><img id="6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd" name="kingdom come 2 square" caption="" alt="" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="661" height="654" attribution="" endorsement="" credit="" class="" /></p></div></div></figure></a><p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/new-pc-games-2026/" target="_blank" data-dimension112="2ee38946-d39d-11f1-aceb-d12e585933c9" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" data-dimension25=""><strong>2026 games</strong></a>: All the upcoming games<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best PC games</strong></a>: Our all-time favorites<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-50-best-free-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Free PC games</strong></a>: Freebie fest<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-fps-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best FPS games</strong></a>: Finest gunplay<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-rpgs-of-all-time/" target="_blank"><strong>Best RPGs</strong></a>: Grand adventures<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-co-op-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best co-op games</strong></a>: Better together</p></div> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/movies-tv/if-kane-parsons-does-make-a-backrooms-sequel-then-itll-be-to-poke-at-aspects-of-the-first-film-that-didnt-feel-like-it-accurately-spoke-to-the-cultural-core-of-backrooms/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ Give me The Bacteria or give me death! ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
                                                                                                                                <guid isPermaLink="false">BLhMaQrGipifqbuyxhfdMA</guid>
                                                                                                <enclosure url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/TccNk3g8FyVnCmsjUMQH89-1920-80.png" type="image/png" length="0"></enclosure>
                                                                        <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 13:35:07 +0000</pubDate>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                <category><![CDATA[Movies & TV]]></category>
                                                                                                                    <dc:creator><![CDATA[ Elie Gould ]]></dc:creator>                                                                                    <dc:source><![CDATA[ https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/5HPuSiRgqza2PQESSqE7gG-320-70.png ]]></dc:source>
                                                                <dc:description><![CDATA[ &lt;p&gt;Elie is a news writer with an unhealthy love of horror games—even though their greatest fear is being chased. When they&#039;re not screaming or hiding, there&#039;s a good chance you&#039;ll find them testing their metal in metroidvanias or just admiring their Pokemon TCG collection. Elie has previously worked at TechRadar Gaming as a staff writer and studied at JOMEC in International Journalism and Documentaries – spending their free time filming short docs about Smash Bros. or any indie game that crossed their path.&lt;/p&gt; ]]></dc:description>
                                                                                                                                <cf:isSponsored>false</cf:isSponsored>
                <cf:hasAffiliateLinks>false</cf:hasAffiliateLinks>
                <cf:isPaid>false</cf:isPaid>
