                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ Over $12 million worth of AI chips seized by Malaysian authorities after suspected smuggling syndicate tries to sneak servers through customs under false pretenses ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p>Malaysian customs have shared this week that $12.11 million worth of AI chips were discovered during an inspection. A total of 30 server units, each featuring cutting edge AI chips, were seized from Kuala Lumpur ​International Airport on October 15.</p><p>The servers originated from and were to be exported to two different, undisclosed Asian countries, <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/china/ai-chips-worth-121-million-seized-malaysia-airport-customs-authority-says-2026-10-28/" target="_blank">according to Reuters</a>. This should have required a trade permit, though the airport's customs director Aspalila Ag Tuah explained this week, "The ‌servers ⁠were falsely declared as electronic components... and shipped through Malaysia to ensure there would be no restrictions imposed while being exported to the next destination."</p><p>A Malaysian firm facilitating the movement of the servers has been called upon to assist in the investigation, though local authorities suspect this goes beyond them. This latest smuggling attempt is thought to be connected to at least five other similar cases involving nearly $25 million worth of chips in total.</p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-OKyGme"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/OKyGme.js" async=""></script><p>The most recent of these cases saw <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/malaysia-customs-seizes-ai-chips-worth-13-mln-kuala-lumpur-airport-2026-06-26/" target="_blank">$13 million worth of chips seized by Malaysian customs back in June</a>—these were also falsely declared as "computer components." Aspalila said that all of these smuggling attempts are suspected to have been carried out by the same syndicate though, as the investigation is still ongoing, they did not share further information.</p><p>To briefly recap, the US has restricted the export of AI chips to a number of global destinations since at least the Biden administration. That's translated to pressure for Malaysia to stop banned AI chips potentially entering China <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/us-pressures-malaysia-to-stop-banned-ai-chips-potentially-entering-china-by-monitoring-every-shipment-that-comes-to-malaysia-when-it-involves-nvidia-chips/" target="_blank">by monitoring 'every shipment that comes to Malaysia when it involves Nvidia chips</a>'.</p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:3840px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="yWtDBKKwdV5uF9DXTMZWrG" name="GettyImages-2148113350" alt="A processed photo of a data center server room, showing racks of computers lit by overhead lights, reflecting off the ground." src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/yWtDBKKwdV5uF9DXTMZWrG-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="3840" height="2160" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: quantic69 via Getty Images)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Simply put, if certain chips can't be directly exported to China, then smugglers will take a more circuitous route with pit stops throughout Asia. For instance last year, Singaporean authorities arrested three men for <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/graphics-cards/group-allegedly-trying-to-smuggle-nvidia-blackwell-chips-stare-down-bail-set-at-over-usd1-million/" target="_blank">a separate smuggling attempt</a>, and earlier this year <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/taiwanese-authorities-detain-nvidia-employee-for-alleged-attempt-to-smuggle-ai-chips-into-china/" target="_blank">Taiwanese authorities detained an Nvidia employee</a> for another alleged smuggling attempt. The US is also concerned that China might have a top-spec chipmaking machine despite export restrictions, <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/the-us-is-worried-china-could-have-a-top-spec-chipmaking-machine-despite-export-restrictions-but-the-machine-maker-asml-denies-it/" target="_blank">but the machine maker ASML denies it</a>.</p><p>With big tech still betting big on AI, <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/nvidia-and-amd-invest-billions-into-meta-as-companies-egg-each-other-on-to-bet-even-more-on-ai/" target="_blank">often to the tune of even more multi-billion dollar investments</a>, it's unsurprising the hardware is in demand worldwide. In fact, more than <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/its-so-much-easier-to-steal-this-than-to-rob-a-bank-usd100-000-000-worth-of-ai-data-center-freight-reportedly-stolen-since-june/" target="_blank">$100,000,000 worth of AI data centre freight has been reportedly stolen</a> within the US alone since June. So long as data centre demand remains high, criminal activity may be likely to follow.</p> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/over-usd12-million-worth-of-ai-chips-seized-by-malaysian-authorities-after-suspected-smuggling-syndicate-tries-to-sneak-servers-through-customs-under-false-pretenses/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ AI continues to be big business, and the impetus behind big crime. ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
                                                                                                                                <guid isPermaLink="false">2iwSyvi2SzQL7PYpBkkwKe</guid>
                                                                                                <enclosure url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/uYyxmMrQ7Kcv3mRXyY2jUT-1920-80.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"></enclosure>
                                                                        <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:49:29 +0000</pubDate>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                <category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
                                                    <category><![CDATA[Software]]></category>
                                                                                                                    <dc:creator><![CDATA[ Jess Kinghorn ]]></dc:creator>                                                                                    <dc:source><![CDATA[ https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/cMDJJibKgeMg3wogzv9AgY-320-70.png ]]></dc:source>
                                                                <dc:description><![CDATA[ &lt;p&gt;Jess has been writing about games for over ten years, spending a significant chunk of that time working on print publications PLAY and Official PlayStation Magazine. When she’s not investigating all things hardware here, she&#039;s either constructing a passionate defence of a 7/10 game, daydreaming about her debut novel, or feeling wistful about the last time she chased some nerds around a field with an oversized foam sword.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; ]]></dc:description>
                                                                                                                                <cf:isSponsored>false</cf:isSponsored>
                <cf:hasAffiliateLinks>false</cf:hasAffiliateLinks>
                <cf:isPaid>false</cf:isPaid>
                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:content type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/uYyxmMrQ7Kcv3mRXyY2jUT-1920-80.jpg">
                                                            <media:credit><![CDATA[Andriy Onufriyenko via Getty Images]]></media:credit>
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:description><![CDATA[Digital generated image of data server.]]></media:description>                                                            <media:text><![CDATA[Digital generated image of data server.]]></media:text>
                                <media:title type="plain"><![CDATA[Digital generated image of data server.]]></media:title>
                                                    </media:content>
                                                    <media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/uYyxmMrQ7Kcv3mRXyY2jUT-1920-80.jpg" />
                                                                                                                                                                    <content:encoded >
                            <![CDATA[
                            <article>
                                <p>Malaysian customs have shared this week that $12.11 million worth of AI chips were discovered during an inspection. A total of 30 server units, each featuring cutting edge AI chips, were seized from Kuala Lumpur ​International Airport on October 15.</p><p>The servers originated from and were to be exported to two different, undisclosed Asian countries, <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/china/ai-chips-worth-121-million-seized-malaysia-airport-customs-authority-says-2026-10-28/" target="_blank">according to Reuters</a>. This should have required a trade permit, though the airport's customs director Aspalila Ag Tuah explained this week, "The ‌servers ⁠were falsely declared as electronic components... and shipped through Malaysia to ensure there would be no restrictions imposed while being exported to the next destination."</p><p>A Malaysian firm facilitating the movement of the servers has been called upon to assist in the investigation, though local authorities suspect this goes beyond them. This latest smuggling attempt is thought to be connected to at least five other similar cases involving nearly $25 million worth of chips in total.</p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-OKyGme"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/OKyGme.js" async=""></script><p>The most recent of these cases saw <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/malaysia-customs-seizes-ai-chips-worth-13-mln-kuala-lumpur-airport-2026-06-26/" target="_blank">$13 million worth of chips seized by Malaysian customs back in June</a>—these were also falsely declared as "computer components." Aspalila said that all of these smuggling attempts are suspected to have been carried out by the same syndicate though, as the investigation is still ongoing, they did not share further information.</p><p>To briefly recap, the US has restricted the export of AI chips to a number of global destinations since at least the Biden administration. That's translated to pressure for Malaysia to stop banned AI chips potentially entering China <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/us-pressures-malaysia-to-stop-banned-ai-chips-potentially-entering-china-by-monitoring-every-shipment-that-comes-to-malaysia-when-it-involves-nvidia-chips/" target="_blank">by monitoring 'every shipment that comes to Malaysia when it involves Nvidia chips</a>'.</p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:3840px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="yWtDBKKwdV5uF9DXTMZWrG" name="GettyImages-2148113350" alt="A processed photo of a data center server room, showing racks of computers lit by overhead lights, reflecting off the ground." src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/yWtDBKKwdV5uF9DXTMZWrG-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="3840" height="2160" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: quantic69 via Getty Images)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Simply put, if certain chips can't be directly exported to China, then smugglers will take a more circuitous route with pit stops throughout Asia. For instance last year, Singaporean authorities arrested three men for <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/graphics-cards/group-allegedly-trying-to-smuggle-nvidia-blackwell-chips-stare-down-bail-set-at-over-usd1-million/" target="_blank">a separate smuggling attempt</a>, and earlier this year <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/taiwanese-authorities-detain-nvidia-employee-for-alleged-attempt-to-smuggle-ai-chips-into-china/" target="_blank">Taiwanese authorities detained an Nvidia employee</a> for another alleged smuggling attempt. The US is also concerned that China might have a top-spec chipmaking machine despite export restrictions, <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/the-us-is-worried-china-could-have-a-top-spec-chipmaking-machine-despite-export-restrictions-but-the-machine-maker-asml-denies-it/" target="_blank">but the machine maker ASML denies it</a>.</p><p>With big tech still betting big on AI, <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/nvidia-and-amd-invest-billions-into-meta-as-companies-egg-each-other-on-to-bet-even-more-on-ai/" target="_blank">often to the tune of even more multi-billion dollar investments</a>, it's unsurprising the hardware is in demand worldwide. In fact, more than <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/its-so-much-easier-to-steal-this-than-to-rob-a-bank-usd100-000-000-worth-of-ai-data-center-freight-reportedly-stolen-since-june/" target="_blank">$100,000,000 worth of AI data centre freight has been reportedly stolen</a> within the US alone since June. So long as data centre demand remains high, criminal activity may be likely to follow.</p>
                                                            </article>
                            ]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                                </item>
                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ 'It’s so much easier to steal this than to rob a bank': $100,000,000+ worth of AI data center freight reportedly stolen since June ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p>AI data centers are springing up across the US at an unprecedented rate, and with them comes a number of problems. Aside from the <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/ny-governor-orders-a-pause-on-large-ai-data-center-construction-to-ensure-that-when-companies-succeed-because-of-new-york-new-yorkers-succeed-too/" target="_blank">noise, disruption, and environmental concerns</a>, the vast infrastructure projects also require the transport of millions of dollars worth of equipment—and opportunistic thieves are cashing in.</p><p><a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/security/grand-theft-ai-highway-thieves-are-stealing-millions-data-center-gear-rcna603733" target="_blank">An NBC News report</a> details two audacious data center equipment heists, both involving literal examples of highway robbery. A shipment carrying $13 million worth of server equipment travelling on a section of interstate through the Mojave Desert had its security escort rammed by a separate vehicle, forcing it to stop. The trailer carrying the equipment was later found at a truck yard in California, minus its valuable load.</p><p>"The accident was not an accident," says Gerado Pachucha, an ex-member of the LA Country Sheriff's Department cargo criminal apprehension team.  "The bad guys wanted that load, so they intentionally took out the escorts."</p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-OKyGme"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/OKyGme.js" async=""></script><p>A second incident, also involving a collision with a security vehicle, led to the loss of another trailer carrying an estimated $10 million worth of server rack components. NBC reports that, according to industry leaders, consultants, and investigators it's spoken to regarding the heists, thieves are estimated to have stolen more than $100 million worth of data center freight since June of this year.</p><p>Keith Lewis, head of operations of a cargo theft tracking team at Verisk CargoNet, says "It’s like stealing pure gold bars."</p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:3840px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="yWtDBKKwdV5uF9DXTMZWrG" name="GettyImages-2148113350" alt="A processed photo of a data center server room, showing racks of computers lit by overhead lights, reflecting off the ground." src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/yWtDBKKwdV5uF9DXTMZWrG-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="3840" height="2160" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: quantic69 via Getty Images)</span></figcaption></figure><p>"We’ve seen some of these thefts go up into 30-plus million dollars for one particular shipment. It’s so much easier to steal this than to rob a bank."</p><p>Aside from the server racks themselves, data centers also require a vast amount of copper wires, fiber optic cables, RAM modules, storage drives, and various other high-value components that need to be transported to their destination by conventional means. </p><p>"Things that are precursors for the AI data centers: copper, server racks and RAM. Those are the things that are the precursors to build one, so that’s what we’re seeing the big increase in," says Lewis. "It’s the dollar value that’s getting the attention because the product they’re stealing costs so much more."</p><p>And despite the addition of security escorts, it seems some criminals are willing to go to increasingly sophisticated lengths to obtain such high-value items.</p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="kNCDGE87fMuTymbxurAHYN" name="GettyImages-1269232851.jpg" alt="A stock photo of a hacker with computers in dark room. The devices are displaying computer code on the screens." src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/kNCDGE87fMuTymbxurAHYN-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: boonchai wedmakawand via Getty Images)</span></figcaption></figure><p>The FBI issued a warning in April of this year regarding "<a href="https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2026/PSA260430" target="_blank">cyber-enabled strategic cargo theft</a>," in which an online heist method is detailed. This involves spoofing broker's email addresses to gain access to cargo systems and bidding on loads, which are then said to be "cross-docked" or "transloaded" to complicit drivers, who then move the cargo to a separate location for resale.</p><p>However, while the two hit-and-run style thefts detailed above are seen as isolated incidents (in terms of their methods) for now, the experts NBC has spoken to view them as a potential escalation of violent tactics more commonly seen in other countries—and it's not just data center equipment that's at risk.</p><p>Scott Cornell, chair of the Transported Asset Protection Association, Americas, says: "These are extremely high-level organized rings that are operating from a dozen different countries, targeting freight in the United States supply chain. These are not unsophisticated crews just pulling off theft."</p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:2560px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="wGYg4gdNXtacCXqv7fc6xM" name="american truck sim rain ext.jpg" alt="Truckin' in the rain." src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/wGYg4gdNXtacCXqv7fc6xM-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="2560" height="1440" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: SCS Software)</span></figcaption></figure><p>"They’re planned. They’re plotted. They’re strategic. They’re playing chess in the supply chain," says Cornell.</p><p>Meanwhile, states such as Arizona, California, Tennessee, and Illinois have established specialised cargo theft task forces to deal with the issue. Private security escort companies are seeing increasing demand from data center logistics providers, while <a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/2853" target="_blank">a new bill to combat wider organized retail crime</a> is currently being debated by US congress.</p><p>Where the money goes, I guess it's no surprise that organised crime will follow. And given the value of some of the data center freight now making its way across the US, it seems that opportunistic criminals have a new high-value target to consider.</p> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/its-so-much-easier-to-steal-this-than-to-rob-a-bank-usd100-000-000-worth-of-ai-data-center-freight-reportedly-stolen-since-june/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ 'It’s like stealing pure gold bars.' ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
                                                                                                                                <guid isPermaLink="false">NDBYLHukcfPq6T4RB36upZ</guid>
                                                                                                <enclosure url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/AF6Trq5u3J38euMTaJVNwk-1920-80.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"></enclosure>
                                                                        <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:53:40 +0000</pubDate>                                                                                                                                <updated>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 17:36:41 +0000</updated>
