                                                </item>
                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ 'Morale has been terrible' at EA, workers claim, saying that AI mandates and mismanagement have left the company 'a cold dead place that's slowly decaying as leadership pilfers every last cent' ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/rip-bioware-ea-has-shut-down-the-legendary-rpg-developer/">EA took the axe to BioWare last week</a> after a long, slow death partially born of mandates to either work on live service games that didn't work (Anthem) or pivot from live service games in messy, chaotic development cycles (Veilguard). A report from <a href="https://www.verticalslice.news/ea-workers-decry-mismanagement-and-corporate-greed-after-mass-layoffs/" target="_blank">Vertical Slice</a> now also claims that its employee morale is at an all-time low.</p><p>Speaking to the site's Chris Kerr, choosing to remain anonymous, sources state that "Morale has been terrible. The line we kept hearing is 'do less, better' but it just felt like we were doing the same amount with less people."</p><p>It doesn't help that "upper management was very excited about Claude and the possibilities of [generative] AI, much to the chagrin of my team." It's not entirely shocking that generative AI is exciting the executive class—and, to give credit where it's perhaps undue, there are places in which the use of AI could, if applied conservatively, save on busywork.</p><p>But in an <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/foolproof-ways-to-get-laid-off-in-the-videogame-industry/">utterly brutal industry environment</a>, none of these employees seem keen on the idea that management might replace them with AI while not understanding their jobs: "The message from every single manager I've heard is 'this is the future, you have to get onboard'," writes one source, while another adds: "They won't tell you these tools are a mandate, but they'll make it impossible or very difficult for you to do your job without [them]."</p><p>Another source in the article claims that there is a sense that "EA executives cannot fail, only be failed. They've been fortunate that [EA Sports FC] sells and makes so much money that it can finance their bad bets." </p><p>That is a pervasive feeling—one does have to wonder why the axe falls first on the employees rather than, say, executive-level pay or bonuses, some of which could fund the yearly salaries of dozens of employees handily. Take Battlefield 6, for instance, <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/after-laying-off-battlefield-6-developers-ea-pays-its-ceo-usd38-million-because-battlefield-6-did-so-well/">which laid off several developers</a> and then paid EA's CEO $38 million for the game's success.</p><p>To further put it in perspective, Andrew Wilson's yearly pay was worth roughly 305 median salaries at the publisher's studios in 2025. </p><p>To return to BioWare for a moment, the report also paints an ugly picture of the mismanagement of that studio. Predictably, the constant cajoling to try and get it to make a live service masterpiece. </p><p>"A lot of money got wasted going down these roads that didn't lead anywhere," says one source, while another writes: "There was never a desire to take risks. Never a desire to do anything interesting. It was just 'here is what these 14 UXR [User Experience Research] studies are saying, so we've got to do it this way."</p><p>Like the cherry on top of a terrible sundae, Vertical Slice's reporting ends with an eminently quotable summation of the entire fiasco: "That the recent email about the organisation changes [sounded like] it was written by a robot best sums up EA. A cold dead place that's slowly decaying as leadership pilfers every last cent, leaving the rest of us to worry about how we feed our families."</p><div class="product"><a data-dimension112="728a73e4-d38e-11f1-86d9-011d8fc46683" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" target="_blank" rel="nofollow"><figure class="van-image-figure "  ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:661px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:98.94%;"><img id="6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd" name="kingdom come 2 square" caption="" alt="" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="661" height="654" attribution="" endorsement="" credit="" class="" /></p></div></div></figure></a><p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/new-pc-games-2026/" target="_blank" data-dimension112="728a73e4-d38e-11f1-86d9-011d8fc46683" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" data-dimension25=""><strong>2026 games</strong></a>: All the upcoming games<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best PC games</strong></a>: Our all-time favorites<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-50-best-free-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Free PC games</strong></a>: Freebie fest<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-fps-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best FPS games</strong></a>: Finest gunplay<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-rpgs-of-all-time/" target="_blank"><strong>Best RPGs</strong></a>: Grand adventures<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-co-op-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best co-op games</strong></a>: Better together</p></div> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/morale-has-been-terrible-at-ea-workers-claim-saying-that-ai-mandates-and-mismanagement-have-left-the-company-a-cold-dead-place-thats-slowly-decaying-as-leadership-pilfers-every-last-cent/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ "EA executives cannot fail, only be failed." ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
                                                                                                                                <guid isPermaLink="false">CZzXCikSuzAeibrBX2iqPN</guid>
                                                                                                <enclosure url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/ZkfRgQoWCNtPFShNDkfMS9-1920-80.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"></enclosure>
                                                                        <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:47:20 +0000</pubDate>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                <category><![CDATA[Gaming Industry]]></category>
                                                                                                <author><![CDATA[ harvey.randall@futurenet.com (Harvey Randall) ]]></author>                    <dc:creator><![CDATA[ Harvey Randall ]]></dc:creator>                                                                                    <dc:source><![CDATA[ https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/Rws7mDGqrkaXrNKCH4jZ2D-320-70.png ]]></dc:source>
                                                                <dc:description><![CDATA[ &lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt; ]]></dc:description>
                                                                                                                                <cf:isSponsored>false</cf:isSponsored>
                <cf:hasAffiliateLinks>false</cf:hasAffiliateLinks>
                <cf:isPaid>false</cf:isPaid>
                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:content type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/ZkfRgQoWCNtPFShNDkfMS9-1920-80.jpg">
                                                            <media:credit><![CDATA[Patrick T. Fallon/Bloomberg via Getty Images]]></media:credit>
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:description><![CDATA[Attendees gather in front of an Electronic Arts Inc. (EA) logo displayed on a screen during the company&#039;s EA Play event ahead of the E3 Electronic Entertainment Expo in Los Angeles, California, U.S., on Saturday, June 10, 2017. EA revealed two new titles along with the annual iterations of the company&#039;s sports games, as well as unveiling the highly anticipated &quot;Star Wars: Battlefront II&quot; open-world multiplayer gameplay. Photographer: Patrick T. Fallon/Bloomberg via Getty Images]]></media:description>                                                            <media:text><![CDATA[Attendees gather in front of an Electronic Arts Inc. (EA) logo displayed on a screen during the company&#039;s EA Play event ahead of the E3 Electronic Entertainment Expo in Los Angeles, California, U.S., on Saturday, June 10, 2017. EA revealed two new titles along with the annual iterations of the company&#039;s sports games, as well as unveiling the highly anticipated &quot;Star Wars: Battlefront II&quot; open-world multiplayer gameplay. Photographer: Patrick T. Fallon/Bloomberg via Getty Images]]></media:text>
                                <media:title type="plain"><![CDATA[Attendees gather in front of an Electronic Arts Inc. (EA) logo displayed on a screen during the company&#039;s EA Play event ahead of the E3 Electronic Entertainment Expo in Los Angeles, California, U.S., on Saturday, June 10, 2017. EA revealed two new titles along with the annual iterations of the company&#039;s sports games, as well as unveiling the highly anticipated &quot;Star Wars: Battlefront II&quot; open-world multiplayer gameplay. Photographer: Patrick T. Fallon/Bloomberg via Getty Images]]></media:title>
                                                    </media:content>
                                                    <media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/ZkfRgQoWCNtPFShNDkfMS9-1920-80.jpg" />
                                                                                                                                                                    <content:encoded >
                            <![CDATA[
                            <article>
                                <p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/rip-bioware-ea-has-shut-down-the-legendary-rpg-developer/">EA took the axe to BioWare last week</a> after a long, slow death partially born of mandates to either work on live service games that didn't work (Anthem) or pivot from live service games in messy, chaotic development cycles (Veilguard). A report from <a href="https://www.verticalslice.news/ea-workers-decry-mismanagement-and-corporate-greed-after-mass-layoffs/" target="_blank">Vertical Slice</a> now also claims that its employee morale is at an all-time low.</p><p>Speaking to the site's Chris Kerr, choosing to remain anonymous, sources state that "Morale has been terrible. The line we kept hearing is 'do less, better' but it just felt like we were doing the same amount with less people."</p><p>It doesn't help that "upper management was very excited about Claude and the possibilities of [generative] AI, much to the chagrin of my team." It's not entirely shocking that generative AI is exciting the executive class—and, to give credit where it's perhaps undue, there are places in which the use of AI could, if applied conservatively, save on busywork.</p><p>But in an <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/foolproof-ways-to-get-laid-off-in-the-videogame-industry/">utterly brutal industry environment</a>, none of these employees seem keen on the idea that management might replace them with AI while not understanding their jobs: "The message from every single manager I've heard is 'this is the future, you have to get onboard'," writes one source, while another adds: "They won't tell you these tools are a mandate, but they'll make it impossible or very difficult for you to do your job without [them]."</p><p>Another source in the article claims that there is a sense that "EA executives cannot fail, only be failed. They've been fortunate that [EA Sports FC] sells and makes so much money that it can finance their bad bets." </p><p>That is a pervasive feeling—one does have to wonder why the axe falls first on the employees rather than, say, executive-level pay or bonuses, some of which could fund the yearly salaries of dozens of employees handily. Take Battlefield 6, for instance, <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/after-laying-off-battlefield-6-developers-ea-pays-its-ceo-usd38-million-because-battlefield-6-did-so-well/">which laid off several developers</a> and then paid EA's CEO $38 million for the game's success.</p><p>To further put it in perspective, Andrew Wilson's yearly pay was worth roughly 305 median salaries at the publisher's studios in 2025. </p><p>To return to BioWare for a moment, the report also paints an ugly picture of the mismanagement of that studio. Predictably, the constant cajoling to try and get it to make a live service masterpiece. </p><p>"A lot of money got wasted going down these roads that didn't lead anywhere," says one source, while another writes: "There was never a desire to take risks. Never a desire to do anything interesting. It was just 'here is what these 14 UXR [User Experience Research] studies are saying, so we've got to do it this way."</p><p>Like the cherry on top of a terrible sundae, Vertical Slice's reporting ends with an eminently quotable summation of the entire fiasco: "That the recent email about the organisation changes [sounded like] it was written by a robot best sums up EA. A cold dead place that's slowly decaying as leadership pilfers every last cent, leaving the rest of us to worry about how we feed our families."</p><div class="product"><a data-dimension112="728a73e4-d38e-11f1-86d9-011d8fc46683" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" target="_blank" rel="nofollow"><figure class="van-image-figure "  ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:661px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:98.94%;"><img id="6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd" name="kingdom come 2 square" caption="" alt="" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/6offQUY4CXebir2TC27dMd-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="661" height="654" attribution="" endorsement="" credit="" class="" /></p></div></div></figure></a><p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/new-pc-games-2026/" target="_blank" data-dimension112="728a73e4-d38e-11f1-86d9-011d8fc46683" data-action="Deal Block" data-label="2026 games" data-dimension48="2026 games" data-dimension25=""><strong>2026 games</strong></a>: All the upcoming games<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best PC games</strong></a>: Our all-time favorites<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-50-best-free-pc-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Free PC games</strong></a>: Freebie fest<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-fps-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best FPS games</strong></a>: Finest gunplay<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/best-rpgs-of-all-time/" target="_blank"><strong>Best RPGs</strong></a>: Grand adventures<br /><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/the-best-co-op-games/" target="_blank"><strong>Best co-op games</strong></a>: Better together</p></div>
                                                            </article>
                            ]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                                </item>
                                <item>
                                                            <title><![CDATA[ Phantom Blade Zero dev says don't get the latest Nvidia drivers ]]></title>
                                                                                                <dc:content><![CDATA[ <p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/action/phantom-blade-zero-vs-onimusha-way-of-the-sword/" target="_blank">Phantom Blade Zero</a>, the highly anticipated wuxia action RPG, is finally out in the world, and if you're planning on playing it on an Nvidia graphics card, maybe consider skipping the latest Nvidia driver update. Yes, that's the opposite of developer <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/phantom-blade-zero-devs-say-the-game-needs-300-gb-of-free-space-for-the-pre-load-process-alongside-updated-system-requirements/" target="_blank">S-Game's pre-launch advice</a>. </p><p>Today, the <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/news/app/4115450/view/698779962638860721" target="_blank">Phantom Blade Zero devs took to the Steam page </a>with a post titled "Reason and Madness" recounting the history of the dev team, previous works, and how Phantom Blade Zero came to be. This is followed up with three announcements: a recommendation to download the 5 GB day one patch, encouragement to try the game on handhelds and lower-end/mid-range PCs, and, finally, advice not to use the latest Nvidia driver, 621.01. </p><p>It says, "Based on our testing, version 617.42 currently offers the best experience in Phantom Blade Zero. If you’ve installed a driver newer than 617.42, we recommend rolling back to 617.42 and choosing the clean installation option. Please stay on this version until Nvidia releases its next driver update, which we expect to arrive soon."</p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-OKyGme"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/OKyGme.js" async=""></script><p>Game Ready Driver 621.01 launched on Wednesday, October 21, and, in addition to providing some bug fixes, it is intended to better support DLSS and RTX in games like Phantom Blade Zero, Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 4, and NBA 2K27. </p><p>The Phantom Blade Zero team does not elaborate on how the previous drivers are better than the current one, but the recommendation implies it's significant enough to negatively impact your experience. </p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="HxHYfD2JnfBBDNmAPbsm6T" name="phantom-blade-zero" alt="Phantom Blade Zero - Dancing lion breathing fire" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/HxHYfD2JnfBBDNmAPbsm6T-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: S-Games Studios)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Luckily, it's very easy to roll back Nvidia drivers in the Nvidia App. Simply boot it up, click on 'Drivers', then scroll down to whatever previous driver you want to roll back to, click the three dots on the side, and then click to install. </p><p>If you don't want to install the Nvidia app, you can roll back drivers by opening up the Device Manager, clicking on "Display Adapters", then right-clicking on your GPU, hitting "Properties", navigating to the "Driver" tab and then rolling back drivers there. </p><p>We don't yet have a timeline on when Nvidia's next driver is due to be released, but S-Game seems to think it will be soon, so you can always hold out for that if you won't be playing the game in the near future. </p> ]]></dc:content>
                                                                                                                                            <link>https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/phantom-blade-zero-dev-says-dont-get-the-latest-nvidia-drivers/</link>
                                                                            <description>
                            <![CDATA[ Luckily, rolling back drivers is pretty easy. ]]>
                                                                                                            </description>
                                                                                                                                <guid isPermaLink="false">g96iteCiAbpidJxSsRYPJC</guid>
                                                                                                <enclosure url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/9NgfHRH4G9SjYg3JJ7mozS-1920-80.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"></enclosure>
                                                                        <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:36:07 +0000</pubDate>                                                                                                                                <updated>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:52:45 +0000</updated>
                                                                                                                                            <category><![CDATA[Hardware]]></category>
                                                                                                                    <dc:creator><![CDATA[ James Bentley ]]></dc:creator>                                                                                    <dc:source><![CDATA[ https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/PVsHAkx27zJptZHndizEAE-320-70.png ]]></dc:source>
                                                                <dc:description><![CDATA[ &lt;p&gt;James is a more recent PC gaming convert, often admiring graphics cards, cases, and motherboards from afar. It was not until 2019, after just finishing a degree in law and media, that they decided to throw out the last few years of education, build their PC, and start writing about gaming instead. In that time, he has covered the latest doodads, contraptions, and gismos, and loved every second of it. Hey, it’s better than writing case briefs.&lt;/p&gt; ]]></dc:description>
                                                                                                                                <cf:isSponsored>false</cf:isSponsored>
                <cf:hasAffiliateLinks>false</cf:hasAffiliateLinks>
                <cf:isPaid>false</cf:isPaid>
                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:content type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/9NgfHRH4G9SjYg3JJ7mozS-1920-80.jpg">
                                                            <media:credit><![CDATA[S-Game Studios]]></media:credit>
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    <media:description><![CDATA[Phantom Blade Zero - Soul]]></media:description>                                                            <media:text><![CDATA[Phantom Blade Zero - Soul]]></media:text>
                                <media:title type="plain"><![CDATA[Phantom Blade Zero - Soul]]></media:title>
                                                    </media:content>
                                                    <media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/9NgfHRH4G9SjYg3JJ7mozS-1920-80.jpg" />
                                                                                                                                                                    <content:encoded >
                            <![CDATA[
                            <article>
                                <p><a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/action/phantom-blade-zero-vs-onimusha-way-of-the-sword/" target="_blank">Phantom Blade Zero</a>, the highly anticipated wuxia action RPG, is finally out in the world, and if you're planning on playing it on an Nvidia graphics card, maybe consider skipping the latest Nvidia driver update. Yes, that's the opposite of developer <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/hardware/phantom-blade-zero-devs-say-the-game-needs-300-gb-of-free-space-for-the-pre-load-process-alongside-updated-system-requirements/" target="_blank">S-Game's pre-launch advice</a>. </p><p>Today, the <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/news/app/4115450/view/698779962638860721" target="_blank">Phantom Blade Zero devs took to the Steam page </a>with a post titled "Reason and Madness" recounting the history of the dev team, previous works, and how Phantom Blade Zero came to be. This is followed up with three announcements: a recommendation to download the 5 GB day one patch, encouragement to try the game on handhelds and lower-end/mid-range PCs, and, finally, advice not to use the latest Nvidia driver, 621.01. </p><p>It says, "Based on our testing, version 617.42 currently offers the best experience in Phantom Blade Zero. If you’ve installed a driver newer than 617.42, we recommend rolling back to 617.42 and choosing the clean installation option. Please stay on this version until Nvidia releases its next driver update, which we expect to arrive soon."</p><div style="min-height: 250px;">                                <div class="kwizly-quiz kwizly-OKyGme"></div>                            </div>                            <script src="https://kwizly.com/embed/OKyGme.js" async=""></script><p>Game Ready Driver 621.01 launched on Wednesday, October 21, and, in addition to providing some bug fixes, it is intended to better support DLSS and RTX in games like Phantom Blade Zero, Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 4, and NBA 2K27. </p><p>The Phantom Blade Zero team does not elaborate on how the previous drivers are better than the current one, but the recommendation implies it's significant enough to negatively impact your experience. </p><figure class="van-image-figure  inline-layout" data-bordeaux-image-check="" ><div class='image-full-width-wrapper'><div class='image-widthsetter' style="max-width:1920px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"><img id="HxHYfD2JnfBBDNmAPbsm6T" name="phantom-blade-zero" alt="Phantom Blade Zero - Dancing lion breathing fire" src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/HxHYfD2JnfBBDNmAPbsm6T-1920-80.jpg" mos="" align="middle" fullscreen="" width="1920" height="1080" attribution="" endorsement="" class="inline" /></p></div></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description" class=" inline-layout"><span class="credit" itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: S-Games Studios)</span></figcaption></figure><p>Luckily, it's very easy to roll back Nvidia drivers in the Nvidia App. Simply boot it up, click on 'Drivers', then scroll down to whatever previous driver you want to roll back to, click the three dots on the side, and then click to install. </p><p>If you don't want to install the Nvidia app, you can roll back drivers by opening up the Device Manager, clicking on "Display Adapters", then right-clicking on your GPU, hitting "Properties", navigating to the "Driver" tab and then rolling back drivers there. </p><p>We don't yet have a timeline on when Nvidia's next driver is due to be released, but S-Game seems to think it will be soon, so you can always hold out for that if you won't be playing the game in the near future. </p>
                                                            </article>
