                                                    <media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/mGAbwHEYqU99h2xFrdszPL-1920-80.jpg" />
                                                                                                                                                                    <content:encoded >
                            <![CDATA[
                            <article>
                                <p>Grand Theft Auto 6's NPCs are so incredibly complicated, it briefly <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/action/gta-6-was-delayed-partially-because-the-amount-of-npcs-was-horrifying-and-required-a-dedicated-studio-full-of-writers-to-handle/">sent the studio into a bit of a "horrifying"</a> spin, requiring an entire team of writers and producers to be assembled just to handle the workload. This has since been doubly confirmed by the studio in a recent <a href="https://www.gamesradar.com/edge/gtavi-cover/">mega-preview from our friends at Edge</a>:</p><p>"This same philosophy governs the streets," the preview reads, "Rather than relying chiefly on procedural systems to assemble interchangeable crowds, Rockstar established an entire Los Angeles-based team of writers, directors and producers devoted to making Leonida's NPCs more individually realised."</p><p>According to the feature, that meant creating an absurd number of animations: "The studio art-directed every [NPC], creating around 600,000 animations."</p><p>What's more, "these pedestrians are assigned personality traits that help determine how they respond: temperament, aggression, where they are, what they have seen, and how the player has behaved towards them. Different districts have their own demographic and professional textures."</p><p>As for what a "professional texture" actually is, Rockstar North co-studio head Rob Nelson goes into more detail: "Who's going to be aggressive? How many people are going to go for a weapon? How many are going to test me?" Nelson says, repeating the same self affirmations I make daily in the mirror.</p><p>The preview continues: "The old GTA pedestrian, a kind of moving prop waiting to panic when the player points a gun, has here splintered into a kaleidoscopic range of possible reactions. Back on the sidewalk, that abstraction suddenly becomes practical. The man with the dog has decided that Jason's stare constitutes a test. </p><p>"He squares up, then unclips the leash from the animal's collar. Nelson turns and runs. The dog gives chase. A flock of pigeons bursts into the air. Finally, the animal loses interest and trots back to its owner. Jason slows to a walk, selects a pack of cigarettes from his inventory, lights one, takes a drag, and continues down the street."</p><figure role="gallery"><figure><img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/T9i3ELY2SbwqjddTenqBxK-1920-80.jpg" alt="GTA 6 location Ambrosia" /><figcaption><small role="credit">Rockstar Games</small></figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/rjLFde7bmaQCWqYowbJvaD-1920-80.jpg" alt="GTA 6 character Real Dimez" /><figcaption><small role="credit">Rockstar Games</small></figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/YH3iSkA5ibnzmxVCCUR2LZ-1920-80.jpg" alt="GTA 6 Vice City screenshot" /><figcaption><small role="credit">Rockstar Games</small></figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/kKdLdoqBqEoLHHRF5ECvGa-1920-80.jpg" alt="GTA 6 Vice City screenshot" /><figcaption><small role="credit">Rockstar Games</small></figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/cvwjuWSQa3pRDSHyG2roXo-1920-80.jpg" alt="GTA 6 Leonida Keys screenshot" /><figcaption><small role="credit">Rockstar Games</small></figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/WZFiWSGdnEmxuRKVaKB37-1920-80.jpg" alt="GTA 6 Leonida Keys screenshot" /><figcaption><small role="credit">Rockstar Games</small></figcaption></figure></figure><p>Really, the way the preview puts it, GTA 6 seems so thoroughly complicated you could legitimately just sit there and people-watch for a bit: "On the opposite side of the road, Nelson spots an arrest taking place. A suspect is being cuffed by two police officers, who have left their vehicles unattended. He sneaks up to one, clicks the boot open, pulls out a shotgun and slings it onto his back, before walking on, leaving a puff of cigarette smoke curling in the air. We have been watching for three minutes."</p><p>Is this sort of immersive sim, top-end simulation particularly necessary in a videogame that, in its purest form, is about wildly driving vehicles and committing crimes? If you're Rockstar, clearly it is, and I can't say I'm not impressed by the amount of craft reportedly on display here. We'll see how it all holds up when millions of people are playing.</p><p>On console, that is—we're still waiting to hear about a GTA 6 PC launch date. <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/games/grand-theft-auto/report-claims-grand-theft-auto-6-will-launch-on-pc-sooner-than-anyone-expected/">Rumor has it</a> that the wait won't be too long, but we're not holding our breath.</p><p>You can read <a href="https://www.gamesradar.com/edge/gtavi-cover/">Edge's GTA 6 feature story over at GamesRadar</a>.</p>
